\subsection{Three-Environment Threshold Sensitivity}
\label{sec:three_env_sensitivity}

To assess threshold robustness across environments, we evaluate the $1.5\times$ NVE threshold on a focused factorial subset of $n{=}26$ configurations spanning Walker Walk, Cheetah Run, and Cartpole Swingup ($n_{\text{pos}}{=}7$, $n_{\text{neg}}{=}19$; Table~\ref{tab:nve_threshold_sensitivity}). This analysis excludes intervention experiments (SGS, RAVE, TCN, dual-critic) to isolate the core diagnostic question: does trained $\dt$-embedding predict CIVO? Cartpole Swingup serves as a non-CIVO negative control: trained $\dt$-embedding does not trigger CIVO (NVE ${<}1.0\times$, 0/3 seeds), consistent with environment-specificity to multi-joint locomotion (\S\ref{sec:f3f5_summary}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{NVE threshold sensitivity for CIVO detection across three environments ($n{=}26$: 7 CIVO-positive, 19 negative). AUROC${=}0.970$. The $1.5\times$ threshold (bold) maximizes F1 with perfect recall. FP at $1.5\times$: exp\_047 (frozen, $1.71\times$) and exp\_057 (frozen, $1.88\times$), both transient self-correcting spikes.}
\label{tab:nve_threshold_sensitivity}
\footnotesize
\begin{tabular}{ccccccccc}
\toprule
Threshold & TP & FP & TN & FN & Precision & Recall & F1 & Accuracy \\
\midrule
$1.3\times$ & 7 & 4 & 15 & 0 & 0.636 & 1.000 & 0.778 & 0.846 \\
\rowcolor{gray!15}
$1.5\times$ & 7 & 2 & 17 & 0 & 0.778 & 1.000 & \textbf{0.875} & 0.923 \\
$1.7\times$ & 5 & 2 & 17 & 2 & 0.714 & 0.714 & 0.714 & 0.846 \\
$2.0\times$ & 5 & 0 & 19 & 2 & 1.000 & 0.714 & 0.833 & 0.923 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The $1.5\times$ threshold achieves AUROC${=}0.970$ with perfect recall (7/7 positives detected) and F1${=}0.875$. The two false positives are frozen-embedding configurations (exp\_047, NVE${=}1.71\times$; exp\_057, NVE${=}1.88\times$), both exhibiting transient value spikes that self-correct within 20K steps---consistent with the mechanistic distinction between gradient-driven CIVO (trained embedding) and transient noise (frozen embedding). Classification is robust across $[1.3\times, 2.0\times]$: lowering to $1.3\times$ adds two false positives (one frozen, one no-embedding configuration with NVE${=}1.41\times$) without gaining true positives; raising to $2.0\times$ eliminates all false positives but sacrifices recall (5/7, losing two borderline cases at $1.53\times$ and $1.62\times$).

\subsection{NVE Normalization Robustness}
\label{sec:nve_robustness}

